# SRG-APP-000875-AU-000380  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must for public key-based authentication, implement
# a local cache of revocation data to support path discovery and validation.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000875-AU-000380
group:           V-263581
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004068
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    Splunk can hold revocation data locally and consult it during certificate
                 validation: server.conf [sslConfig] certificateStatusValidationMethod =
                 crl turns on certificate status validation for every Splunk TLS channel
                 (Splunk Web, splunkd, and Splunk-to-Splunk), and server.conf [kvstore]
                 sslCRLPath names the local PEM certificate revocation list file, the
                 product's CRL directory being $SPLUNK_HOME/auth/crl. The spec records that
                 splunkd uses the CRL file when the instance runs in Common Criteria or
                 FIPS mode, or when certificate status validation is configured.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.

check:           server.conf [sslConfig] certificateStatusValidationMethod equals crl
check:           server.conf [kvstore] sslCRLPath non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [kvstore] sslCRLPath https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] certificateStatusValidationMethod https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Retrieval and refresh of the revocation list from the issuing CA's
                 distribution point - carried by a scheduled operating-system job or the
                 PKI's distribution process; the product consumes PEM CRL files placed for
                 it and does not fetch or renew them.
external:        OCSP-based status checking, where the organization requires it in addition
                 to CRLs - not offered by the product; carried by a PKI-terminating proxy
                 or the host platform.
external:        Certification path discovery beyond the configured trust anchor - carried
                 by the organization's PKI and the trust anchor supplied through the
                 sslRootCAPath settings.
external:        Storage and protection of the cached revocation files - carried by the
                 host operating system's filesystem.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/server.conf.spec:714 ([sslConfig]), :1125
                 (certificateStatusValidationMethod = crl, only accepted value, default
                 empty, CRL files in $SPLUNK_HOME/auth/crl), :5232 ([kvstore]), :5392
                 (sslCRLPath, PEM format, default empty - no revocation list)
finding:         The application keeps no local cache of revocation data - server.conf
                 [sslConfig] certificateStatusValidationMethod is not set to "crl" and/or
                 [kvstore] sslCRLPath is empty, so a revoked certificate is accepted during
                 path validation; this determination is from the perspective of the
                 application layer alone, the retrieval and refresh of the revocation list
                 and any OCSP checking being carried outside the application.
